WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.330
An estimator is a rule that uses sample data to estimate a population quantity

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:09.295
The sample mean averages the observed values

00:00:09.295 --> 00:00:17.767
For eight, ten, and twelve, the estimate is ten, but the unknown population mean need not equal that particular result.

00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:22.598
A different sample usually produces a different mean

00:00:22.598 --> 00:00:28.337
This variation is part of the estimation problem, not necessarily a calculation mistake

00:00:28.337 --> 00:00:35.400
We judge an estimator by how it behaves across repeated samples under a stated sampling model.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:48.020
For independent observations with a common population variance, the sample mean's standard error is population standard deviation divided by square root of sample size

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:55.567
With standard deviation six, nine observations give standard error two, while thirty-six give standard error one.

00:00:56.867 --> 00:00:59.642
Precision and bias describe different features

00:00:59.642 --> 00:01:07.044
Estimates can cluster tightly around the wrong target if the sampling method systematically favors certain observations

00:01:07.044 --> 00:01:15.833
Increasing the sample size may shrink random error while leaving that systematic shift untouched; design must address the bias.
